Adversaries may manipulate software dependencies and development tools prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise. Applications 
often depend on external software to function properly. Popular open source projects that are used as dependencies in many applications, such as pip and NPM packages, 
may be targeted as a means to add malicious code to users of the dependency.

RISK:

An attacker could compromise third-party dependencies, package repositories, build tools, source code repositories, CI/CD pipelines, or developer workstations/tools and insert malicious code into trusted software. That code may then be built, signed, deployed, or distributed as legitimate software, leading to compromise of internal systems, customers, or both.

Level of Urgency is High due to the threat of the following factors:

	• Ability to bypass normal security controls
	• Malware will spread widely across applications and environments
	• Difficult to detect until after deployment
	• Affect both your organization and downstream customers

Impact

	• Unauthorized access to systems or data
	• Remote code execution or malware delivery
	• Credential theft and privilege escalation
	• Data exfiltration
	• Service disruption / outages
	• Integrity loss of released software
	• Regulatory, legal, financial, and reputational damage

Threat Hypothesis

The Threat Actor is hosting and publishing malicious node package manager (NPM) software packages on popular code repositories like GitHub. Developers have installed the malicious packages on their corporate workstations and the JavaScript has harvested secrets to be used for credential access.



